      "name": "clinical_complexity_score",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A graded complexity axis is genuinely useful for triage/scheduling analysis, giving a new dimension beyond urgency. However the scale is opaque and some values are hard to justify: the acne case scored 0 while a viral URI scored 6 despite the URI being clinically simpler, and the MI follow-up scored only 4. The ordering is not consistently warranted by the source text, indicating some ungrounded/invented precision."
    },
    {
      "name": "triage_priority",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A Low/Medium/High priority is directly relevant to the triage query. It is somewhat redundant with the existing 'urgency' column, reducing leverage, but it adds a slightly finer reading (e.g. routine MI follow-up bumped to Medium). Values are reasonable readings of urgency plus clinical content."
    },
    {
      "name": "visit_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Categorising visits as Preventive/Acute/Chronic_Management/General gives a strong grouping axis for scheduling policy (e.g. chronic mgmt vs acute). Sample values are well grounded: annual physical=Preventive, MI follow-up=Chronic_Management, knee injury=Acute."
    },
    {
      "name": "telehealth_eligible",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Highly relevant to scheduling improvement (shifting suitable visits to telehealth). Binary but decision-actionable. Values are defensible inferences: physical exam and stable MI follow-up flagged Yes, injury needing exam/MRI flagged No, though eligibility is a judgment the text does not explicitly state."
    },
    {
      "name": "duration_deviation_min",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Deviation of actual vs expected duration is very useful for scheduling accuracy analysis. But the expected/recommended baseline is itself model-derived, so the deviation values are only as grounded as that assumption; heavy concentration at 0 suggests it is often computed rather than observed. Partly ungrounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "recommended_slot_length_min",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly actionable for scheduling. However it maps almost one-to-one onto triage_priority (Low->15, Medium->30, High->45), making it largely a restatement of that column, which caps its added leverage. Values are reasonable but derivative."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
  "source_groundedness": 0.72,
  "rationale": "The added columns collectively give several genuinely useful new axes (visit_type, telehealth_eligible, complexity, deviation) for a triage/scheduling analysis, with only modest redundancy (triage_priority vs urgency; recommended_slot_length vs triage_priority). Groundedness is generally acceptable for the categorical columns, which read the source text soundly, but weaker for the numeric scores (clinical_complexity_score and duration_deviation_min) where the values assert a precision and baseline the source text does not clearly support, and some complexity orderings are inconsistent with clinical content."
